00:16:59,520 --> 00:17:05,200
Yeah, on, so on the growth marketing side, we, as I mentioned before, we wanted to get traction.

244
00:17:05,600 --> 00:17:10,880
There's a great quote by, I think, Sam Altman, right, which is, Momentum is like oxygen for a startup.

245
00:17:11,040 --> 00:17:13,680
So you want to get as much momentum as soon as possible.

246
00:17:14,080 --> 00:17:18,960
And I know in some way I'm stating the obvious, but there are very clear trade-offs, right?

247
00:17:19,280 --> 00:17:21,600
In some cases, you delay a launch.

248
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,720
In some cases, you want to perfect certain flows.

249
00:17:25,360 --> 00:17:26,480
or perfect this and that.

250
00:17:26,800 --> 00:17:29,440
Maybe you delay your launch because of hiring, whatever.

251
00:17:30,080 --> 00:17:38,160
I think the thing that we got really, really right from the start is we wanted to get as much momentum as possible as quickly as possible because momentum is like oxygen.

252
00:17:38,400 --> 00:17:45,840
You just feed off of that momentum and it lets you put more and more energy and more and more cash if you need to into the company to keep it growing.

253
00:17:46,480 --> 00:17:51,360
So in the early days, it was very obvious that we were going to use social media to grow.

254
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:59,280
I think we live in this really interesting time where it does not matter, generally speaking, how many followers you have on short form video.

255
00:18:00,160 --> 00:18:01,680
It just matters if you make great content.

256
00:18:02,240 --> 00:18:04,560
And so we were trying everything in the beginning.

