00:21:19,440 --> 00:21:20,800
It's like you have to give that away.

314
00:21:21,120 --> 00:21:28,960
I think too many people, again, it's like, yeah, I mean, maybe, maybe Zach having gone through a venture backed, you kind of had the model of like, capital equals growth.

315
00:21:29,120 --> 00:21:40,160
Because I think I see sometimes with bootstrap, David's bootstrapped a lot of things, but like because the, um, you know, dipping below the zero is into your personal money, like there tends to be a real hesitation, right?

316
00:21:40,400 --> 00:21:42,400
And that it's almost it's almost easier to bet.

317
00:21:42,640 --> 00:21:44,080
I mean, it's why a lot of people always venture.

318
00:21:44,240 --> 00:21:47,440
I don't think it's a bad reason to, it's like it's easier to spend someone else's money.

319
00:21:47,600 --> 00:21:48,320
It really is, right?

320
00:21:48,560 --> 00:21:49,920
And there's good and bad things about that.

321
00:21:50,080 --> 00:21:58,240
The good things being is like you're a little bit credited protected or whatever and all those, but like, you know, from the bootstrapper side, I think sometimes, you know, like people are hesitant.

322
00:21:58,560 --> 00:22:00,960
And not even in like I'm going to spend 100k my own money.

323
00:22:01,120 --> 00:22:02,160
Like that's a big swing.

324
00:22:02,640 --> 00:22:09,600
But even like, oh, I can't even ship a product with negative gross margins or even like, even like risk my gross margins because, oh my God, what's going to happen, right?

325
00:22:09,840 --> 00:22:12,800
And I think that can make you you make some really suboptimal decisions, right?

326
00:22:13,040 --> 00:22:14,880
If you're too concerned about it with it too early.

327
00:22:15,280 --> 00:22:17,280
Almost everything gets cheaper at scale, right?

328
00:22:17,920 --> 00:22:20,560
As you get bigger, one, you figure out how to make it cheaper.

329
00:22:21,120 --> 00:22:23,840
Models are deflationary, like our models are getting cheaper.

