00:22:23,920 --> 00:22:26,800
So even like today's functionality will be just half the cost in a year.

331
00:22:27,360 --> 00:22:32,000
When the time comes, yeah, it's a flip side in computer science, they would say, no premature optimization.

332
00:22:32,320 --> 00:22:33,680
That's totally true in companies too.

333
00:22:33,840 --> 00:22:35,680
Like, don't optimize at the beginning.

334
00:22:35,920 --> 00:22:36,800
So that's really interesting.

335
00:22:36,960 --> 00:22:44,400
So you guys had like very low COGS, maybe higher than like an app that didn't have any AI backing, but not something that was a concern.

336
00:22:45,040 --> 00:23:15,760
And then you just started the flywheel with a little bit of your own cash to like get it going and then and then did it just was it just like pull out reinvest pull out reinvest like cycle all the way up was it was it fairly smooth or how did the how did the scaling go yeah yeah i would say so um we were able to just uh continue working out of the the app store revenue stripe as well we have an android app now and One interesting thing is that like the audio transcription costs are actually more than the LLM generally.

337
00:23:16,160 --> 00:23:18,480
And so that's like one that might be surprising.

338
00:23:19,200 --> 00:23:21,680
But even there, like the local models are getting really good.

339
00:23:21,840 --> 00:23:24,000
And I think that if we were starting from scratch, we could probably use the.

340
00:23:24,160 --> 00:23:38,080
Yeah, I mean, I guess it's like you only need you need a you need good transcription quality not like perfect maybe right like especially if the LM is probably you know it's gonna know it's gonna know the professor didn't say fish right or something like that's gonna do a pretty good job of like fixing that I would imagine.

341
00:23:38,400 --> 00:23:48,560
Jacob, one one thing to add on to this in terms of the finances is that we were very mindful of the financial you know structure from basically day one.

342
00:23:48,960 --> 00:24:00,880
We knew we'd have to invest some upfront and it was you know around ten thousand maybe each that we had to invest upfront to get things off the ground but pretty much every month from the inception to the acquisition.

343
00:24:00,880 --> 00:24:04,080
And so, in relation to the acquisition, we were around fifty percent EBITDA margins.

344
00:24:04,480 --> 00:24:06,240
And so we really tried to be lean.

345
00:24:06,640 --> 00:24:12,000
And then to your point around raising or reinvesting, there are absolutely trade-offs.

346
00:24:12,160 --> 00:24:17,120
One of the reasons why I think we're so excited to now be under Quizlet is that we can think longer term, right?

347
00:24:17,600 --> 00:24:21,600
We can make longer term decisions without this, you know, make belief in the mouth.

348
00:24:21,680 --> 00:24:22,960
In the mouth.

349
00:24:23,280 --> 00:24:23,840
Yeah, exactly.

350
00:24:24,400 --> 00:24:30,560
And so I think your ability to think long term is definitely influenced by your capital structure, your capital situation.

351
00:24:31,040 --> 00:24:32,640
And so I'm very happy to have had that.

352
00:24:32,720 --> 00:24:35,920
And then on the other side of it, right, it is really nice to have the cash flow.

353
00:24:36,240 --> 00:24:40,160
So that financial discipline, I think, led us to have cash flow kind of throughout, which was nice.

354
00:24:40,560 --> 00:24:43,840
Let's go back to process, though, because I think a lot of folks will be interested.

355
00:24:43,920 --> 00:24:45,440
So you each put in around 10 K.

356
00:24:46,480 --> 00:24:48,640
How did you reach out to those first creators?

357
00:24:48,880 --> 00:24:52,480
Were you just finding people on TikTok and then DMing them?

358
00:24:53,280 --> 00:24:55,280
And were those early ones paid?

359
00:24:55,520 --> 00:24:56,720
Did you do any kind of referral?

360
00:24:56,960 --> 00:24:58,880
Like, how did you get that flywheel going?

361
00:24:59,040 --> 00:25:03,200
Because so many people listening to this podcast, you know, are trying to figure that out.

362
00:25:03,680 --> 00:25:08,320
It was an absolute grind in the beginning, because you're just trying to find the channel that works, right?

363
00:25:08,640 --> 00:25:12,800
And then you just want to exploit may not be the right word, but you want to double down.

364
00:25:13,280 --> 00:25:18,160
and you really want to spam more or less that channel until you reach the limit.

365
00:25:18,560 --> 00:25:20,160
We were reaching out, all of the above, David.

366
00:25:20,480 --> 00:25:24,080
So we were reaching out to creators through if they had an email in their bio.

367
00:25:25,200 --> 00:25:26,720
Funny observation here.

368
00:25:27,520 --> 00:25:32,720
If you reach out to a content creator, I have my opinions on influencers that we can circle back to.

369
00:25:32,960 --> 00:25:42,400
But if you reach out to a content creator and their email is, you know, it's at sunsetagency dot com or something, any agency sounding, you're too late.

370
00:25:43,120 --> 00:25:47,280
Those agencies exist to eliminate the alpha that you're trying to create.

371
00:25:47,520 --> 00:25:47,600
Okay.

372
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:55,280
So they are almost always going to take more than they give in terms of the value that they provide to the company.

373
00:25:55,600 --> 00:25:57,360
There are always exceptions, one hundred percent.

374
00:25:57,760 --> 00:26:00,000
But generally speaking, that's been my observation.

375
00:26:00,240 --> 00:26:06,240
Probably a lot of like mid tier creators too who like hear, Oh, I have an agency and they get it and it's actually like value destructive for them.

376
00:26:06,640 --> 00:26:06,800
Totally.

377
00:26:06,960 --> 00:26:07,760
Yeah, absolutely.

378
00:26:08,160 --> 00:26:14,400
If they have a Gmail in their bio and they have, you know, five, ten K followers, good engagement, that's great.

379
00:26:14,640 --> 00:26:15,840
That's a really nice sweet spot.

380
00:26:16,080 --> 00:26:17,520
And these numbers are very flexible.

381
00:26:17,760 --> 00:26:24,720
But whenever social media switched over from a follower graph to an interest graph and with the four U page and algorithmic distribution.

382
00:26:25,280 --> 00:26:28,080
The number of followers you have matters less and less, right?

383
00:26:28,400 --> 00:26:30,640
The quality of the content you create matters a ton.

384
00:26:31,040 --> 00:26:37,040
So at Scout, my last startup, the product studio, our first TikTok got eight million views.

385
00:26:37,440 --> 00:26:40,640
We had never posted to the account before, but we got eight million views.

386
00:26:40,800 --> 00:26:43,680
It drove 150,000 signups over a two day period.

387
00:26:44,240 --> 00:26:50,320
I think at that time, this was 2021, if we were an app, not a website, I think we would have been number one in the app store.

388
00:26:50,800 --> 00:26:55,760
And so I learned the value of great content posted on TikTok at that time.

389
00:26:56,320 --> 00:26:57,840
And so we knew that that would be the case.

390
00:26:58,000 --> 00:26:59,680
It was just a matter of how do we get there?

391
00:27:00,240 --> 00:27:02,480
So that's what you meant about content creator.

392
00:27:03,120 --> 00:27:05,760
I noticed you chose your words very carefully there.

393
00:27:06,240 --> 00:27:08,000
Content creator, not influencer.

394
00:27:08,240 --> 00:27:12,480
So the strategy wasn't to find an influencer, how to find an audience.

395
00:27:12,720 --> 00:27:15,600
The strategy was to find a content creator who produces great content.

396
00:27:15,920 --> 00:27:16,640
100%.

397
00:27:16,640 --> 00:27:16,800
Yeah.

398
00:27:16,960 --> 00:27:18,160
I couldn't have said it better myself.

399
00:27:18,400 --> 00:27:27,920
One of the struggles with UGC and this playbook is generating content that converts versus generating content that just gets a bunch of views.

400
00:27:28,480 --> 00:27:40,400
And then depending on how you're paying the creators, if you're paying on a view basis, they can really rack up the bill, you know, doing just funny TikToks that get attention but then don't convert.

401
00:27:40,960 --> 00:27:50,560
How did you address that both financially and then also in kind of learning what did convert and kind of incentivizing that kind of content?

402
00:27:51,280 --> 00:27:52,640
Yeah, a couple of things come to mind.

403
00:27:52,960 --> 00:27:55,760
One is being hands on with the content creation team.

404
00:27:56,400 --> 00:28:02,720
So today our content team is about 25 people, 25 part-time creators that are all contractors.

405
00:28:03,360 --> 00:28:11,840
Throughout the lifespan of Cocono pre-acquisition, we were between 5 and 10, maybe at the very most, we had 12 content creators.

406
00:28:12,240 --> 00:28:14,000
And so we kept a very small team.

407
00:28:14,560 --> 00:28:15,920
I worked with everyone closely.

408
00:28:16,080 --> 00:28:17,840
I met with everyone one to one.

409
00:28:18,320 --> 00:28:27,440
I would say some of our competitors and some people or some founders that are building out similar creator programs approach it very, very differently.

410
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:35,840
So we always had the mentality, great quote by Brian Chesky, who I look up to very much as a design background CEO.

411
00:28:36,480 --> 00:28:39,440
He says, we want to build the Navy SEALs, not the Navy.

412
00:28:40,080 --> 00:28:43,920
And we had that approach with our team overall and especially on the content team.

413
00:28:44,480 --> 00:28:46,880
So get to know every single one of the creators.

414
00:28:47,040 --> 00:28:53,680
Make sure you're enabling them with what creative actually works and letting them know what converts.

