00:44:46,960 --> 00:44:49,600
And the daughter's just starting college and her mom's there with her.

605
00:44:50,080 --> 00:45:02,880
And I said, Hey, I would like you to go offer a note taker, something that's going to help improve your grades and, you know, make sure you never miss a detail in lecture, et cetera, help you study, be a personalized study coach, et cetera.

606
00:45:03,520 --> 00:45:05,520
And it's $130 to do it.

607
00:45:05,840 --> 00:45:06,800
Who are you going to ask?

608
00:45:07,120 --> 00:45:09,280
I think the obvious answer is you're going to ask the mom, right?

609
00:45:09,600 --> 00:45:13,120
And so early on we were very keen on marketing to moms.

610
00:45:13,280 --> 00:45:15,200
I think we've since taken a step back.

611
00:45:15,440 --> 00:45:17,360
And by the way, these are not mutually exclusive.

612
00:45:17,520 --> 00:45:18,400
There's a big overlap.

613
00:45:18,640 --> 00:45:21,040
It's finding who the buyer and the most intent is, right?

614
00:45:21,200 --> 00:45:24,720
And you can have good marketing to whoever might be in need, right?

615
00:45:24,960 --> 00:45:32,320
I wonder how many app developers get stuck down the route of, oh, I have to like advertise to the primary user of the product, right?

616
00:45:32,560 --> 00:45:35,360
And that may or may not be the decision maker in a purchase.

617
00:45:35,680 --> 00:45:40,560
David, to your broader question around monetization, just two quick things to add on that are a little bit higher level.

618
00:45:41,360 --> 00:45:44,320
One, great majority of our customers are lifelong learners.

619
00:45:44,800 --> 00:45:47,360
And so they may be quote unquote non-traditional students.

620
00:45:47,600 --> 00:45:56,880
Brett and I have talked to a lot of our customers that are not in the quote unquote traditional 18 to 22 or 23 year old college student age.

621
00:45:57,360 --> 00:46:01,120
They could be studying for something that's not at an accredited university.

622
00:46:01,360 --> 00:46:04,080
They could be at a university but outside of that age range.

623
00:46:04,400 --> 00:46:06,320
Any number of derivatives there.

624
00:46:06,880 --> 00:46:09,680
Always amazing to talk to customers that are just making it happen.

625
00:46:10,480 --> 00:46:11,680
Second thing is that I'm very glad.

626
00:46:11,920 --> 00:46:17,440
Again, it's not very tactical, but I'm very glad we ignored traditional conventional wisdom.

627
00:46:18,640 --> 00:46:26,080
I have a lot of smart friends that if I would have gone to with the idea for Coconut, they would have said, Zach, no one's going to buy this, right?

628
00:46:26,320 --> 00:46:28,080
You're targeting college students, right?

629
00:46:28,640 --> 00:46:31,120
And I'm so glad we didn't do that.

630
00:46:31,840 --> 00:46:39,680
I think in the early days of an idea, you can almost imagine it like a seed and you want to like shelter it from a lot of harm, right?

631
00:46:40,000 --> 00:46:41,520
So that it can at least sprout.

632
00:46:41,920 --> 00:46:45,040
Because in hindsight, it's like, oh, this was amazing, congrats.

633
00:46:45,680 --> 00:46:48,720
But only with the benefit of hindsight did those smart people say that.

634
00:46:54,640 --> 00:46:58,560
I think a much better framework is what happens if it does work, right?

635
00:46:58,800 --> 00:47:00,400
What impact can we have on our customers?

636
00:47:00,720 --> 00:47:02,480
What economic value can we create, et cetera?

637
00:47:02,640 --> 00:47:03,680
So I'm very glad we did that.

638
00:47:04,080 --> 00:47:10,800
Yeah, I mean, that's exactly why I asked the question and what I would have thought externally, you know, just having heard from so many.

639
00:47:11,120 --> 00:47:13,200
But it's like, I mean, it's like my app, a weather app.

640
00:47:13,440 --> 00:47:15,360
Like college students are not going to pay for a weather app.

641
00:47:15,520 --> 00:47:22,000
And so a lot of the conventional wisdom around what people will and won't pay for is formed on totally different products.

642
00:47:22,160 --> 00:47:30,320
And so when you come to the market with a unique value prop with a brand new product that's enabled by AI in a way that you know you could never create this product before.

643
00:47:30,480 --> 00:47:36,480
It's like you you've unlocked demand for that in a way that nobody else had and can charge a premium for it.

644
00:47:36,640 --> 00:47:37,760
So yeah, it's really cool.

645
00:47:38,560 --> 00:47:49,120
One thing on there is like we are aware that like it's expensive and that there are there are students at the end of the day and we want Coconote to be available to as many students as possible.

646
00:47:50,000 --> 00:47:57,360
And it kind of goes back to what Zach was saying earlier around being able to think longer term under Quizlet and stay tuned on on that.

647
00:47:57,680 --> 00:48:00,800
But I think that we'll be able to get Coconote into more people's hands in the future.

648
00:48:01,440 --> 00:48:02,080
That's awesome.

649
00:48:02,960 --> 00:48:09,680
One of the keys in converting folks who saw this UGC video is nailing the onboarding.

650
00:48:10,080 --> 00:48:18,640
What were some of the lessons you learned in getting people in and convincing them of the value and eventually getting them to start that free trial?

651
00:48:19,120 --> 00:48:34,560
Yeah, I think that there's kind of like a meta at this point for consumer apps of have a long onboarding and then hit them with a hard paywall or basically get someone to start a trial within the first session, honestly, if not 24 hours.

652
00:48:36,320 --> 00:48:41,600
We have always had like a decent onboarding, but we hadn't spent a ton of time optimizing it.

653
00:48:42,080 --> 00:48:46,160
And we ran a test trying to basically like double the length.

654
00:48:46,320 --> 00:48:49,120
I think our onboarding is 15 screens or something.

655
00:48:49,440 --> 00:48:53,520
I remember when we started talking to Quizlet team, that's something that they commented on.

656
00:48:53,680 --> 00:48:54,480
I mean, it really works.

657
00:48:54,640 --> 00:49:01,600
Like it really works to get people a bit more invested into what they're doing and feel like it's more personalized, show them some social proof.

658
00:49:02,240 --> 00:49:08,400
And it increased our trial start by 16%, which we already felt like it was pretty good.

659
00:49:08,560 --> 00:49:10,880
And so to continue to raise it was awesome.

660
00:49:10,960 --> 00:49:12,800
And it's one of those things that continues to come back.

661
00:49:13,680 --> 00:49:41,600
What's funny is like as an engineer, whenever you start a new product, you kind of like build the login screen first and because you kind of need it right to start playing around with things and then it ends up staying there throughout the rest of the the product but one of the nice things about being like there's not always a lot of nice things about being in Apple and Google's walls but uh one of the nice things is you actually don't really need to create an account to make a purchase because you're already signed into your Apple or Google account.

662
00:49:42,160 --> 00:49:48,160
So I think the biggest win that we had was moving login to after the paywall basically at the end of onboarding.

663
00:49:49,360 --> 00:49:53,040
That's like, there's some, I think revenuecat dot com makes this pretty easy.

664
00:49:53,360 --> 00:49:54,240
But that's tricky, right?

665
00:49:54,400 --> 00:49:59,120
Like being able to take a payment before you have an identity marker, being able to associate those.

666
00:49:59,360 --> 00:50:01,840
Like there's a bunch of plumbing we do to make that possible.

667
00:50:02,480 --> 00:50:16,960
And that introduces a lot of complexity now, because now, which is there, no matter where you put it, is that like Apple purchase state and like the account state are like not the same thing, which isn't the case when you control the entire like purchase payments flow, like you would if you were doing Stripe on the web or something like this.

668
00:50:17,440 --> 00:50:18,480
It is a big advantage, right?

669
00:50:18,720 --> 00:50:19,920
You know, users don't have to think about it.

670
00:50:20,000 --> 00:50:21,200
You're not pulling out a credit card.

671
00:50:21,280 --> 00:50:22,480
It's like they know it's secure.

672
00:50:22,800 --> 00:50:22,880
Yeah.

673
00:50:23,040 --> 00:50:24,800
They've already done the double tap to pay.

674
00:50:25,120 --> 00:50:25,520
at that point.

675
00:50:25,680 --> 00:50:27,200
So they'll double tap to sign in.

676
00:50:27,440 --> 00:50:32,240
I think we were seeing like 10% drop off by having login being the very first screen.

677
00:50:32,480 --> 00:50:34,400
Were you capturing any contact details before that?

678
00:50:34,720 --> 00:50:39,120
No, this is just like you download the app and open it and now you're forced to create an account.

679
00:50:39,600 --> 00:50:49,040
Well, once people do start that free trial and that's a hard paywall, keeping them and having them convert is that next big key.

680
00:50:49,520 --> 00:50:57,520
What were the unlocks there for helping people convert and any stats around your trial start and trial conversion rates that you want to share?

681
00:50:57,680 --> 00:51:00,720
I feel like web and e commerce have had a really big lead.

682
00:51:01,440 --> 00:51:07,760
when it comes to this stuff, like the real like optimizations and the benefits of being like outside of Apple's walls.

683
00:51:08,480 --> 00:51:10,480
It's more like the wild west of what you can do.

684
00:51:11,680 --> 00:51:20,320
We wanted to try some cancellation flows there on our website and there's like the standard asking questions about why they're canceling things like that.

685
00:51:20,880 --> 00:51:27,760
And we were able to retain like 25% of people from canceling, which we felt like was pretty big.

686
00:51:28,000 --> 00:51:30,000
And to do that, we tried three things.

687
00:51:30,320 --> 00:51:32,400
One of them is like your standard discount offers.

688
00:51:32,640 --> 00:51:35,360
You get 30% off if you stay or something like that.

689
00:51:35,680 --> 00:51:36,640
Another one is pausing.

690
00:51:37,280 --> 00:51:43,840
So because we have students as our users, they come May or June, they don't want to keep paying over the summer.

691
00:51:44,080 --> 00:51:46,320
And so we thought we could try pausing for three months.

692
00:51:46,800 --> 00:51:49,040
And then the last one was trial extensions.

693
00:51:49,920 --> 00:51:51,680
And this is one that I haven't seen that much.

694
00:51:52,320 --> 00:51:59,680
But basically, if you're on a free trial and you had a cancel, then we basically say, do you just need more time and offer them seven more days?

695
00:52:00,400 --> 00:52:00,800
And that was...

696
00:52:01,360 --> 00:52:16,320
the most successful one by far and it really keeps people on that auto renew and I think it's so hard to get them back onto auto renew once they once they leave but that was a big one for us and And I think there's a way to actually do this on Apple and Google now too.

697
00:52:16,800 --> 00:52:18,720
And so I'm going to be exploring that.

698
00:52:19,040 --> 00:52:22,160
Apple does now have it's in it's in limited beta right now.

699
00:52:22,400 --> 00:52:24,480
You have to ask for it and get approved and everything like that.

700
00:52:24,720 --> 00:52:28,320
But it's retention offers in app store settings.

701
00:52:28,480 --> 00:52:35,440
Like when they're actually outside of your app trying to cancel, you can now like show a screen and offer a discount and things like that.

702
00:52:35,600 --> 00:52:38,640
I don't actually know off the top of my head what all you can do.

703
00:52:38,800 --> 00:52:40,560
I think it's only a discount.

704
00:52:40,800 --> 00:52:43,280
I don't think you can add additional trial.

705
00:52:43,600 --> 00:52:45,680
Are you all doing anything of that inside the app as well?

706
00:52:45,920 --> 00:52:54,240
Like do you have inside settings where people go to to to cancellation inside the app or is that only on the web that you're seeing those wins and doing that?

707
00:52:54,640 --> 00:52:55,360
It's only on the web.

708
00:52:55,840 --> 00:53:00,000
It's also in the app if you subscribe through Stripe and then we can just show it in a web view.

709
00:53:00,160 --> 00:53:03,200
But yeah, that's the one that I still want to bring to mobile.

710
00:53:03,360 --> 00:53:06,640
Yeah, it's harder to do with Apple Payments, but worth trying.

711
00:53:06,960 --> 00:53:11,200
Well, as we wrap up, I did want to like actually talk through the acquisition.

712
00:53:12,800 --> 00:53:14,400
How did that start?

713
00:53:15,040 --> 00:53:16,000
What was the process?

714
00:53:16,400 --> 00:53:17,280
Did they reach out to you?

715
00:53:17,440 --> 00:53:18,240
You reached out to them?

716
00:53:18,560 --> 00:53:21,200
Did you talk to a banker about selling a broker?

717
00:53:21,840 --> 00:53:22,400
What was the process?

718
00:53:22,880 --> 00:53:24,000
Quizlet actually reached out.

719
00:53:24,000 --> 00:53:29,280
So I would say we were only a few months old and we were talking with Quizlet.

720
00:53:29,440 --> 00:53:33,600
I remember when Brett first brought it to me, he was like, hey, you know, someone reached out.

721
00:53:34,320 --> 00:53:35,200
Should we take this call?

722
00:53:35,600 --> 00:53:38,560
And I said, you know, I'd rather not just say we're heads down building.

723
00:53:38,720 --> 00:53:41,920
We had been around for a couple months, like, what could possibly come of that?

724
00:53:42,480 --> 00:53:47,600
And I think Brett literally slept on it and he came back and said, I'd really like to take the call.

725
00:53:47,840 --> 00:53:50,080
Like, let's just see what, you know, what's going on.

726
00:53:50,640 --> 00:53:51,040
I don't know.

727
00:53:51,200 --> 00:53:53,120
Some people know what they want to do with the company when they started it.

728
00:53:53,280 --> 00:53:53,840
Some people don't.

729
00:53:54,000 --> 00:53:55,120
Was this like predisposed at all?

730
00:53:55,200 --> 00:53:56,880
Did you be like, hey, we want to build this and sell this?

731
00:53:56,960 --> 00:53:57,920
Hey, we just want to see this?

732
00:53:58,000 --> 00:54:00,800
Did you have those discussions or was it more open-ended?

733
00:54:01,120 --> 00:54:04,320
I would say we had a healthy outlook and we didn't have our blinders on.

734
00:54:04,560 --> 00:54:06,800
We had conversations with a handful of companies.

735
00:54:07,200 --> 00:54:12,480
Eventually, when we kind of committed to going down this path, it was taking up a good bit of time.

736
00:54:12,720 --> 00:54:23,440
And that's, you know, the one thing I would say to other founders, if you're getting inbound and you're really weighing the options here of acquisition, I would say, make sure you keep your eye on the ball.

737
00:54:23,680 --> 00:54:29,840
Because the moment you take your eye off the ball, they're going to, that will reflect negatively in the ongoing conversations, we'll say that.

738
00:54:30,000 --> 00:54:36,960
And so the moment we kind of said, hey, we're going to take this seriously, but we want to keep our energy focused on building and growing the company.

739
00:54:38,240 --> 00:54:39,600
We worked with an investment bank.

740
00:54:40,000 --> 00:54:44,400
We worked with a partner named Chris Park at Accretive Partners, and they were really great to work with.

741
00:54:45,040 --> 00:54:47,040
And then they kind of led the process from there.

742
00:54:47,280 --> 00:54:51,040
So we actually got back in touch with Quizlet through the investment baker.

743
00:54:51,280 --> 00:54:58,240
So we built a relationship with Quizlet, and we really loved, I think there's a lot of mission overlap for what it's worth.

744
00:54:58,400 --> 00:55:09,200
Something that we haven't covered is that Coconote's mission is to give learners superpowers, and that is not the explicit mission of Quizlet, but I would say in spirit it is very, very similar, right?

745
00:55:09,600 --> 00:55:10,960
Larners can be of all ages.

746
00:55:11,040 --> 00:55:17,840
For me, one of the things that I love love about our mission statement is that we acknowledge on the superpowers front, it is intentionally vague.

747
00:55:18,720 --> 00:55:25,920
The foundational LLM, you know, or AI model providers are doing amazing work and we have no right to compete with them.

748
00:55:26,160 --> 00:55:36,480
And so we want to recognize that and our job is to basically tailor the foundational work that is being done, the amazing foundational work, turn it into a superpower for learners.

749
00:55:36,800 --> 00:55:43,600
And so while that isn't the explicit mission of Quizlet, it's very much in spirit, I would say, the mission in some way, shape or form of Quizlet.

750
00:55:43,760 --> 00:55:45,360
And so we were able to think much longer term.

751
00:55:45,920 --> 00:55:49,760
A lot of things kind of overlapped and thankfully the deal closed in December of last year.

752
00:55:50,160 --> 00:55:50,240
Yeah.

753
00:55:50,480 --> 00:55:57,840
I mean, and I have to imagine just having like mission alignment also is like, I mean, I've spoken as a Quizlet user and like, you know, a watcher for a long time.

754
00:55:58,000 --> 00:55:58,800
It's a great company, right?

755
00:55:58,960 --> 00:55:59,520
They have a good brand.

756
00:55:59,680 --> 00:56:00,640
They have good products, right?

757
00:56:00,800 --> 00:56:05,600
Uh, makes it a lot easier to kind of swallow the idea of like your baby going somewhere else.

758
00:56:05,840 --> 00:56:12,720
Um, and obviously like the integration work, which I'm sure you're doing now, and like all that stuff is like, you know, it's not just a dollar in very rare cases.

759
00:56:12,880 --> 00:56:15,600
It's a dollar's a dollar's in a signature and it's over, right?

760
00:56:15,760 --> 00:56:18,160
Like there's a partnership that will take many years, right?

761
00:56:18,320 --> 00:56:27,280
As this plays out, but like from, okay, we're going to do this or we're seriously thinking about it to like close the deal, like how long did that last for you guys?

762
00:56:27,680 --> 00:56:31,520
It was kind of running in the background throughout the year, throughout last year.

763
00:56:31,840 --> 00:56:33,440
I mean, there's there's different like phases.

764
00:56:33,920 --> 00:56:37,120
There's the talking phase, then there's the Netflixing phase, right?

765
00:56:37,360 --> 00:56:37,440
Yeah.

766
00:56:38,160 --> 00:56:38,960
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

767
00:56:39,440 --> 00:56:48,480
There's that, but also with the banker, like the bankers preparing things on their end and stuff like that where we're not really like actively doing anything and they're just getting ready to reach out to people.

768
00:56:49,280 --> 00:56:52,720
So it was, um, it felt like a long time to be honest.

769
00:56:53,200 --> 00:56:53,840
It's a long time.

770
00:56:54,080 --> 00:56:56,480
Like your whole company's two years and change, right?

771
00:56:56,640 --> 00:56:57,520
So like, right.

772
00:56:57,840 --> 00:56:58,480
It's half of it.

773
00:56:58,720 --> 00:56:59,360
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

774
00:56:59,600 --> 00:57:01,920
Chris was kind of our third co-founder a little bit.

775
00:57:03,920 --> 00:57:04,240
That's great.

776
00:57:04,400 --> 00:57:10,800
Tell me if I'm cutting too deep, but did you keep it to between the two of you or did like the rest of the team know or like how did you manage that for that period of time?

777
00:57:11,200 --> 00:57:12,720
Yeah, yeah, we kept it between us.

778
00:57:12,880 --> 00:57:21,600
It felt like the kind of thing where, you know, we ourselves were not banking on anything happening, you know, like we didn't want to feel like it was in the bag or anything like that.

779
00:57:21,760 --> 00:57:22,880
And so we wouldn't want to share it.

780
00:57:23,040 --> 00:57:25,200
Yeah, it can be a real distraction.

781
00:57:25,360 --> 00:57:31,280
Like just literally, like you start counting your money or whatever, you know, even if, especially if you don't know what the deal is going to be shaped like, right?

782
00:57:31,440 --> 00:57:33,040
You just start imagining and whatever.

783
00:57:33,680 --> 00:57:36,800
I mean, even sure for you guys, it was, even if you managed it well, probably took a lot of.

784
00:57:36,960 --> 00:57:40,240
of mental fortitude to kind of keep it compartmentalized and keep leading the business.

785
00:57:40,480 --> 00:57:44,240
And so you managed to keep growing throughout that whole period that you were very happy with.

786
00:57:44,480 --> 00:57:45,040
Yeah, totally.

787
00:57:45,280 --> 00:57:49,600
I don't think we have the metrics pulled up, but but revenue grew quite a bit.

788
00:57:49,760 --> 00:57:52,240
I would say roughly doubled throughout that time.

789
00:57:52,640 --> 00:57:54,320
Jacob, I think you, you may appreciate this.

790
00:57:54,640 --> 00:57:56,960
My wife didn't even know, much less the folks on the TV.

791
00:57:57,360 --> 00:57:58,240
Wow, that's a commitment.

792
00:57:58,320 --> 00:57:58,400
Yeah.

793
00:57:59,040 --> 00:58:00,800
I would never be able to hold that line.

794
00:58:02,160 --> 00:58:07,760
And so, yeah, she, you know, she knew we were talking with Quizlet, but she had no knowledge of, you know, where the process stood.

795
00:58:07,920 --> 00:58:09,040
I think that's smart.

796
00:58:09,280 --> 00:58:11,360
If you're not letting it leak out of a part of your mind, right?

797
00:58:11,680 --> 00:58:12,800
You're just being like, for sure.

798
00:58:12,800 --> 00:58:15,600
You're just being compartmentalized and being like, hey, this is just this little thing.

799
00:58:15,680 --> 00:58:17,680
I'm not going to treat it like a thing until it's real, right?

800
00:58:18,080 --> 00:58:28,320
And getting a spouse overly excited and like, it's hard enough as an individual not letting it distract you of like, oh, the money's coming and like, what am I going to do with the money?

801
00:58:28,480 --> 00:58:30,240
And like, oh, it's going to be such a relief.

802
00:58:30,400 --> 00:58:33,120
I mean, there's so many like emotions tied to it.

803
00:58:33,680 --> 00:58:38,400
And then with another person, then all their emotions get tied to it.

804
00:58:38,560 --> 00:58:42,320
Any baggage they have about like money and security and everything else like that.

805
00:58:42,480 --> 00:58:44,880
Like, it's a very emotional thing.

806
00:58:45,200 --> 00:58:47,600
Okay, Zach, did they find out like after close?

